 | `cancel` | `id, fence, error, at` | Job `cancelled` |
-| `requeue` | `id, at` | Dead job `ready` again, attempts back to 0 |
-| `snapshot` | `at, seq, jobs, ...` | Replaces all state. Always the first record of a compacted log |
+| `release` | `id, fence, error, at` | Lease given up because its worker died: job `ready` again at once. `error` is the reason |
+| `requeue` | `id, at` | Dead job `ready` again, attempts back to 0. The fence is kept |
+| `snapshot` | `at, seq, completionSeq, jobs, idem` | Replaces all state. Always the first record of a compacted log |
+
+A record that is not about the job's current attempt has no effect when replayed: `complete`, `fail`, `expire`, `release`, `dead` and `heartbeat` need the job to be leased with that `fence`, `claim` needs it `ready`, `requeue` needs it `dead`, and `cancel` needs it `ready` or leased. The time a retry is due is always the record's `at` plus its `delayMs`, never the time of the replay.
+
+Each job in a snapshot carries every field a job has (`priority`, `attempts`, `fence`, `completionSeq`, and the rest). `idem` lists the remembered keys, `{ key, id, at, status, result }`, including keys whose job has been pruned. A snapshot without `idem` is rebuilt from its jobs.
 
 A reader ignores record types it doesn't know. Older logs may lack newer fields: `priority` counts as 0, and an older snapshot may lack `attempts`, `fence` and `idem`.
 
